findings.body:
Password-reset tokens must come from a CSPRNG. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256 bits) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is (1) a non-cryptographic PRNG and (2) at most 10¹² distinct values (~40 bits), often only 9 base36 characters after `padStart`. An attacker can brute-force reset links or predict tokens and take over accounts.

For edge runtimes, use Web Crypto instead of `Math.random`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

`crypto.getRandomValues` is available on Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge, Deno, and modern Node, and preserves the original 256-bit strength.
